content.text:
I’d use a small, conservative set—after extracting only `error`, `message`, or `detail` from a JSON body:

```ts
const SECRET_FIELD =
  /(\b(?:authorization|proxy-authorization|api[-_ ]?key|access[-_ ]?token|refresh[-_ ]?token|client[-_ ]?secret|password|passwd|secret|credential|signature)\b\s*[:=]\s*)(?:"[^"]*"|'[^']*'|[^\s,;}"]+)/gi;

const COOKIE = /\b(?:cookie|set-cookie)\s*[:=][^\r\n]*/gi;

const BEARER = /\bBearer\s+[A-Za-z0-9._~+/=-]+/gi;

const JWT =
  /\beyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{5,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{5,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{5,}\b/g;

const SECRET_QUERY =
  /([?&](?:api[-_]?key|access[-_]?token|refresh[-_]?token|token|signature|password|secret)=)[^&#\s]+/gi;

const UUID =
  /\b[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[1-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[89ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}\b/gi;
```

Application order:

```ts
function sanitize(text: string): string {
  return text
    .slice(0, 4096)
    .replace(COOKIE, '[REDACTED]')
    .replace(SECRET_FIELD, '$1[REDACTED]')
    .replace(BEARER, 'Bearer [REDACTED]')
    .replace(JWT, '[JWT_REDACTED]')
    .replace(SECRET_QUERY, '$1[REDACTED]')
    .replace(UUID, '[ID_REDACTED]')
    .replace(/[\u0000-\u001f\u007f]+/g, ' ')
    .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
    .trim()
    .slice(0, 256);
}
```

I would **not** use a broad “any long alphanumeric string” regex—it would hide useful Hevy validation details and can over-redact. Regexes are only defense-in-depth; the primary protection is extracting a small allowlist of known error fields.

